      "content": "If you were married 10+ years and divorced, you may have a retirement benefit you don't know about 💸\n\nDivorced-Spouse Social Security: Complete Guide\n\nWho qualifies:\n→ Married for at least 10 years\n→ Currently divorced (not remarried — though if new marriage ends, prior benefit returns)\n→ Age 62 or older\n→ Not entitled to a higher benefit on your own work record\n\nThe benefit:\n→ Up to 50% of your ex-spouse's full retirement benefit (their PIA)\n→ At YOUR full retirement age: 50%\n→ At 62 (earliest): ~32.5%\n→ If your own earned benefit > 50% of theirs: you receive whichever is higher\n\nWhat most people don't know:\n→ Your ex does NOT need to know\n→ Your ex does NOT need to consent\n→ The SSA does NOT notify your ex when you file\n→ Zero effect on their monthly benefit\n→ Zero effect on their current spouse's benefit\n→ Multiple ex-spouses can claim simultaneously — zero reduction for anyone\n→ The pool doesn't diminish\n\nYour ex just needs to be ELIGIBLE (age 62+) — not necessarily claiming yet\n→ If divorced 2+ years, you can file even if ex hasn't filed\n\nDivorced survivor benefit (if ex dies):\n→ Up to 100% of their benefit at your full retirement age\n→ Same 10-year marriage requirement\n→ Often larger than the living spousal benefit\n→ Can switch between your own and survivor benefit for maximum payout\n\nCheck eligibility:\n→ ssa.gov — free, 10 minutes\n→ Financial planners: among the most commonly unclaimed benefits in America\n\n#moneyfacts #socialsecurity #divorcedspousal #retirement #personalfinance #financialliteracy #retirementplanning #divorcefinances #womenandmoney #financialrights",
